<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<manifest>
  <!--
    The version here is what the Power Platform upgrades on, and the release
    workflow refuses to publish a tag that disagrees with it. Bump this in the
    same commit as the tag.
  -->
  <control namespace="PCFHub"
           constructor="LinkColumn"
           version="0.1.0"
           display-name-key="LinkColumn_Name"
           description-key="LinkColumn_Desc"
           control-type="standard">

    <!--
      Leave this disabled unless the control calls a third-party service.

      `enabled="true"` makes the control **premium**, and premium is a cost
      somebody else pays: every end user of an app containing this component
      then needs a Power Apps licence rather than an Office 365 one. Nothing
      fails to build, nothing warns at install, and the person who finds out is
      not the person who typed it — so this is worth confirming with whoever
      owns the control rather than flipping to make a `fetch` work.

      When it is genuinely enabled, list every domain. `<domain>` children are
      declarative and can be listed under `enabled="false"` too, which is the
      schema's own second example; it is the `enabled` attribute alone that
      carries the licensing consequence.

          <external-service-usage enabled="true">
            <domain>tile.example.com</domain>
          </external-service-usage>

      `npm run check` fails on `enabled="true"` with no domain, and warns on
      `enabled="true"` at all.
    -->
    <external-service-usage enabled="false" />

    <!--
      A dataset control binds a collection rather than a column.

      This one declares **no `property-set` roles**, which is a decision worth
      making deliberately rather than inheriting:

        Declare roles when the control assigns meaning to specific columns — a
        chip has a label and a colour, a map pin has a latitude and a longitude.
        Each role is a named slot in this file, so the arity is fixed at whatever
        you write here.

        Declare none, and read `dataset.columns`, when the control renders
        whatever it is given. A table is the clearest case: there is no way to
        declare "however many columns the view has", and the metadata a layout
        needs — order, visualSizeFactor, isPrimary, isHidden, disableSorting —
        exists on real view columns and nowhere else.

      If you add roles, add `::props-table{kind=dataset_column}` to docs/api.md.
      With none, leave it out: the directive renders an empty table, which reads
      as "this control has no dataset columns" rather than as a missing section.

      And if you add roles, **change demo/records.json so each column's `alias`
      differs from its `name`.** A role is found on the column by `alias` and
      read off the record by `name`, and getting that backwards renders nothing
      at all against a real view. The fixture here sets both to the same string,
      which is correct with no roles and is exactly what let that bug reach
      production in pcf-tag-list: the demo looked right because either order
      worked against it. Realistic values — `"name": "cr123_tagname"` with
      `"alias": "labelField"` — make the mistake fail where you can see it.

        <data-set name="records" display-name-key="Records_DataSet_Name">
          <property-set name="labelField" display-name-key="LabelField_Name"
                        of-type="SingleLine.Text" usage="bound" required="true" />
        </data-set>

      The other attribute worth knowing is `cds-data-set-options`, which decides
      whether a model-driven subgrid keeps its own chrome. Off by default; add it
      to show any of the three:

        <data-set name="records" display-name-key="Records_DataSet_Name"
                  cds-data-set-options="displayCommandBar:true;displayViewSelector:true;displayQuickFind:true" />

      It belongs on `<data-set>`, not on `<control>` — it configures the host's
      furniture, which sounds like a control-level concern and is not.

      Turn the **command bar** on if the control calls `setSelectedRecordIds()`:
      without it, selection is a value the control reports and nothing acts on.
      The **view selector** is only sensible if the control renders whatever
      columns arrive, since switching view changes them. A control with fixed
      property-set roles usually wants all three off, because its layout does not
      survive the user picking a different view.

      Two traps. The attribute's value is typed as a plain string, so a
      misspelled option compiles clean and silently does nothing. And the
      Microsoft reference marks the attribute `Required: Yes`, which the tooling
      contradicts — it is optional in fact, and absent means off.

      Model-driven only; canvas apps ignore it.
    -->
    <data-set name="records" display-name-key="Records_DataSet_Name" />

    <property name="pageSize"
              display-name-key="PageSize_Name"
              description-key="PageSize_Desc"
              of-type="Whole.None"
              usage="input"
              required="false"
              default-value="25" />

    <!--
      Notified *before* the record is opened, so the value is observable even on
      a host where opening does nothing — which is the canvas case.
    -->
    <property name="openedRecordId"
              display-name-key="OpenedRecordId_Name"
              description-key="OpenedRecordId_Desc"
              of-type="SingleLine.Text"
              usage="output" />

    <resources>
      <code path="index.ts" order="1" />
      <css path="css/LinkColumn.css" order="1" />
      <!--
        Every language the control ships.

        A .resx that exists on disk but is not listed here is not shipped, and
        nothing warns — the file is simply never packed, and the control runs
        in English for that locale while the repository looks translated.
        `npm run check` catches both that and a key missing from a language.

        Drop the ones you will not maintain. A stale translation is worse than
        an honest fallback to English, and every key added to 1033 is a key
        owed to each file listed here.

        THE ORDER IS DELIBERATE AND 1033 GOES LAST. `pcf-start` picks the .resx
        by manifest order rather than by locale: it reads whichever file is
        listed *last* and ignores the LCID and the browser language entirely.
        The platform itself ignores the order completely — a real app resolves
        from the user's provisioned language — so `npm start` is the only thing
        in the world that reads this order, and it is ordered for it. Listing
        1033 first is the tidier-looking arrangement and it makes `npm start`
        render the whole control in Japanese, which reads as a broken build.

        The consequence to remember: ADD A NEW LANGUAGE ABOVE THIS LINE, not
        below it. Appending a sixth file after 1033 silently moves the harness
        to that language, and the symptom shows up in a `npm start` session
        nobody connects to a manifest edit.
      -->
      <resx path="strings/LinkColumn.3082.resx" version="1.0.0" />
      <resx path="strings/LinkColumn.1036.resx" version="1.0.0" />
      <resx path="strings/LinkColumn.1031.resx" version="1.0.0" />
      <resx path="strings/LinkColumn.1041.resx" version="1.0.0" />
      <resx path="strings/LinkColumn.1033.resx" version="1.0.0" />
    </resources>

    <!--
      Uncomment only the features actually used. Each one becomes a permission
      prompt for the maker installing the control.

      Note that `openDatasetItem()` needs none of these — it is a method on the
      dataset itself, not on context.navigation.

    <feature-usage>
      <uses-feature name="Utility" required="true" />
      <uses-feature name="WebAPI" required="true" />
    </feature-usage>
    -->
  </control>
</manifest>
